{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,7]],"date-time":"2026-07-07T07:33:07Z","timestamp":1783409587479,"version":"3.54.6"},"reference-count":75,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"4","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,20]],"date-time":"2023-11-20T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1700438400000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100001461","name":"American Philosophical Society","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100001461","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/100006038","name":"Tinker Foundation","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100006038","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":["nph.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["New Phytologist"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,2]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Summary<\/jats:title><jats:p><jats:list list-type=\"bullet\">\n<jats:list-item><jats:p>The threat of rising global temperatures may be especially pronounced for low\u2010latitude, lowland plant species that have evolved under stable climatic conditions. However, little is known about how these species may acclimate to elevated temperatures. Here, we leveraged a strong, steep thermal gradient along a natural geothermal river to assess the ability of woody plants in the Amazon to acclimate to elevated air temperatures.<\/jats:p><\/jats:list-item>\n<jats:list-item><jats:p>We measured leaf traits in six common tropical woody species along the thermal gradient to investigate whether individuals of these species: acclimate their thermoregulatory traits to maintain stable leaf temperatures despite higher ambient temperatures; acclimate their photosynthetic thermal tolerances to withstand hotter leaf temperatures; and whether acclimation is sufficient to maintain stable leaf thermal safety margins (TSMs) across different growth temperatures.<\/jats:p><\/jats:list-item>\n<jats:list-item><jats:p>Individuals of three species acclimated their thermoregulatory traits, and three species increased their thermal tolerances with growth temperature. However, acclimation was generally insufficient to maintain constant TSMs. Notwithstanding, leaf health was generally consistent across growth temperatures.<\/jats:p><\/jats:list-item>\n<jats:list-item><jats:p>Acclimation in woody Amazonian plants is generally too weak to maintain TSMs at high growth temperatures, supporting previous findings that Amazonian plants will be increasingly vulnerable to thermal stress as temperatures rise.<\/jats:p><\/jats:list-item>\n<\/jats:list><\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111\/nph.19413","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,21]],"date-time":"2023-11-21T21:55:21Z","timestamp":1700603721000},"page":"1447-1463","update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":40,"title":["Leaf thermal safety margins decline at hotter temperatures in a natural warming \u2018experiment\u2019 in the Amazon"],"prefix":"10.1111","volume":"241","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1521-439X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Alyssa T.","family":"Kullberg","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biology University of Miami  Coral Gables FL 33146 USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lauren","family":"Coombs","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hussman Institute of Human Genomics University of Miami Miller School of Medicine  Miami FL 33136 USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Roy D.","family":"Soria Ahuanari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Herbario Regional de Ucayali IVITA, Pucallpa (HRUIP) Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos  Pucallpa 25001 Peru"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4032-7676","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Riley P.","family":"Fortier","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biology University of Miami  Coral Gables FL 33146 USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3618-1144","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kenneth J.","family":"Feeley","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Biology University of Miami  Coral Gables FL 33146 USA"},{"name":"Fairchild Tropical Botanic Garden  Coral Gables FL 33156 USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,20]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_9_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/nph.17640"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1088\/1748-9326\/abe3b9"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_4_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/gcb.14590"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1071\/PP9880263"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_6_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.3732\/ajb.93.1.102"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007\/BF00379886"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.agrformet.2018.07.012"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_9_1","first-page":"20","article-title":"Comparison of NDVI and NDRE indices to detect differences in vegetation and chlorophyll content","volume":"4","author":"Boiarskii B","year":"2019","journal-title":"Journal of Mechanics of Continua and Mathematical Sciences"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_10_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1088\/1748-9326\/7\/2\/024002"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_11_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/nph.16916"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_12_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1093\/jxb\/ert211"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_13_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1002\/jgrg.20067"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_14_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.112